  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>A shop dashboard shows sales. It does not show profit. Production costs sit with the print provider, fees arrive in monthly statements, and refunds and ad charges land weeks later. With several shops and two partners, the question "what can each of us take out this month?" meant an evening in spreadsheets, and the answer was still a guess.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>A private web app with three sections. Company rolls everything up and shows where the money went. Shops has eight tabs covering profit and loss, stores, products, orders, costs, partner payouts and the data behind them. Engine tracks quarterly objectives, a weekly scorecard, who owns each job, and fulfilment and customer-service timing.</p><p>A scheduled sync runs once a day. I designed the interface and wrote all of it, including about 5,000 lines of CSS with no framework and three themes.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>I chose to depend on nothing from the marketplace's API, so sales come from parsing order-notification emails. Layouts differ between shops and change without notice. A sale is accepted only when the email agrees with itself: the subject amount must equal the order total, and the lines must add up to it.</p><p>Anything that fails is counted and shown as missing coverage. Nothing is guessed, and money is held as integer cents everywhere so totals never drift.</p></div>
  </div>

