      <div class="dg-row"><div class="nd big"><b>Postgres ledger</b><small>integer cents · row-level security · audit log</small></div><i class="w"></i><div class="nd big"><b>Metric layer</b><small>what · formula · source · coverage</small></div><i class="w"></i><div class="nd big out"><b>Screens</b><small>Company · Shops · Engine</small></div></div>
      <div class="dg-foot"><span>No marketplace API anywhere in this picture.</span><span>Rejected rows are counted, never dropped.</span></div>
    </div></div>
    <div class="eng-notes">
      <div><h4>An email has to agree with itself</h4><p>Before a sale is stored, four checks run: the subject amount equals the body's order total, the lines add up to that total, item total minus discount equals the subtotal, and the order number matches the invoice link. A failure is recorded under one of eight named reasons.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Whole cents from the first parse</h4><p>A dollar string becomes an integer number of cents once, at the parser. Database columns, sums, margins and the 50/50 split all work on integers, and dollars are formatted only when drawn. Floating-point rounding never touches a payout.</p></div>
      <div><h4>A number knows how complete it is</h4><p>Each metric has one of four states: known, partial, unknown or not applicable. A sum built from 1,562 of 1,604 source rows is marked partial and drawn hatched, with the count stated, so the screen cannot show a confident total it does not have.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Tests at the database, the logic and the screen</h4><p>Row-level security in Postgres decides who can read each row, across 40+ migrations, and 124 pgTAP tests exercise those rules in the database itself. Another 187 app tests cover parsing and maths, and Playwright checks layouts from 320 to 1920 pixels in Chromium and WebKit.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

